{"kind":"report","text":"APPROVED at exact clean head 5bea8c70c91cfcabf1189f1b02aabedd698c00dc, judging artifact 9cb392e6826dad3f7ff236cfb48466528b1ea734.\n\nSelection cannot broaden, which was my main attack. The per-package expression is `^(` + names + `)$` with `regexp.QuoteMeta` on each name, so anchoring prevents a case name matching a longer test and quoting prevents a name being read as a pattern. Go splits `-run` on `/` and this expression has none, so it constrains the top-level name only and every subtest of a named test runs — which is right, since a subtest belongs to the test the case named. `namedTestSelections` splits at `LastIndex(qualified, \".Test\")` and rejects an empty package or test name, so a malformed entry is an error at startup rather than a silently dropped case.\n\nThe inverse holds too, and it is the failure the old reporter had. A named test that is renamed or deleted leaves `-run` matching nothing, `go test` exits zero, and the case lookup misses — so the result is marked `missing`, which is not `pass`, so the case and the report go red. A skipped case is red for the same reason, which is why removing the race skip from the statusview test mattered: a case that quietly skips would otherwise have counted as evidence.\n\nFailures reach the logs rather than a deleted file. A build failure or non-zero exit joins into `runErr`; a malformed JSON stream sets `decodeErr`; either makes the report red, and `failureDetails` is now printed to stderr before the non-zero exit. That closes the specific complaint on dcc02574 — the diagnosis was written into `.spike/SPIKE-RESULTS.md` on a runner that then discarded it, so CI went red twice on README-only commits for reasons nobody could reconstruct.\n\nThe latency split is right, and I checked it against my own bias rather than because I proposed the shape. `TestDepth20000SummarySize` keeps the invariant that actually holds — the depth-20k summary marshals under 64 KiB — with no clock, no HTTP round trip and no race-mode skip. The measurement moved to `BenchmarkWarmDepth20000Summary`, which does not run under `go test` by default, so it can be taken deliberately on an idle machine where it means something. That is the honest disposition of a p99 budget: it is a performance claim, and there is nothing left of it once the clock is removed, so it should not sit in a correctness gate at all.\n\nConfirmed by execution. `go test ./spike/cmd/gitseq-report ./internal/statusview -count=1` passes, with statusview now taking 0.725s against roughly 46s before. Three consecutive `go run ./spike/cmd/gitseq-report` invocations all report `pass`; this morning the same command failed on two of four attempts, and once with every one of its six cases passing above an overall `fail`. The recorded command in evidence.json is the anchored per-package form, so the lane demonstrably runs the named tests and not the module. The worktree is clean after those runs, which is the stable-output condition met by observation rather than assertion.\n\nTwo things worth saying, neither blocking.\n\nThe display name for a missing case uses `strings.Split(key, \".Test\")[1]` while selection uses `LastIndex`. For any real package path these agree; for a path containing `.Test` they would not, and only the label would be wrong. Cosmetic, but the two ought to use the same rule.\n\nThe lane no longer runs the module, which is the point and also a boundary worth stating: it can only speak for the six cases. The test whose flakiness started this — TestWhoamiBoundsStallsAndRejectsRedirects — is not in the case map and will not be exercised here at all. That is correct for an evidence lane, and it means the full-suite coverage of that test now rests entirely on f6383a92 rather than on anything this change provides.\n\nConfirmed by reading only: the missing-case and skip classifications, and the regex semantics for subtests, which I traced rather than instrumented.","body":{"head":"5bea8c70c91cfcabf1189f1b02aabedd698c00dc","verdict":"approved"}}